From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xalpharush <0xalpharush@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] silent reports: close the syslog door too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0014 made the silent runtime write nothing by putting SilentReports()
behind the two doors report text leaves through: ReportFile::Write (everything
Printf(), Report() and RawWrite() funnel through it) and CatastrophicErrorWrite
(CHECK/Die()).  There is a third door it missed.

SharedPrintfCodeNoBuffer (sanitizer_printf.cpp) does not stop at RawWrite().
After the bytes go to the report file it runs two more sinks on the same
buffer: CallPrintfAndReportCallback(buffer) and LogMessageOnPrintf(buffer).
The second is the leak.  On Linux LogMessageOnPrintf, when common_flags()->
log_to_syslog is set, calls WriteToSyslog -> the libc syslog() (sanitizer_linux_
libcdep.cpp), which writes the full report to /dev/log over a path that never
touches ReportFile::Write and so never saw patch 0014's test.  log_to_syslog is
an ordinary common flag: a program (or a downstream __asan_default_options())
can set it, and neither the seal nor the silent mode pins it off.  A silent --
or sealed-and-silent -- binary with log_to_syslog=1 therefore still emits the
whole report, just to syslogd instead of to fd 2.  Audited: a silent build with
log_to_syslog=1 put 3,562 bytes of report on /dev/log while writing nothing to
stderr, which is exactly the writes a silent binary is meant not to make.

The fix guards the runtime-owned sink under the same SilentReports() test the
other two doors use, so the three report exits now agree.  The guard is placed
to draw the line the silent contract draws: LogMessageOnPrintf is the runtime
itself re-emitting its report to a second transport, a runtime-owned write, and
it is suppressed.  CallPrintfAndReportCallback immediately above it is left
alone: it hands the buffer to a callback the *program* installed with
__sanitizer_set_report_fd / set_report_callback, which is program output, not a
runtime write, and silencing it would silence the process rather than the
runtime -- the same boundary patch 0014 draws when it leaves printf and
fprintf(stderr) in the program untouched.  A program that installs a callback
and also wants it silent controls that itself.

Touches sanitizer_common, so this test compiles into every sanitizer's archive,
the same scope as patch 0014's two guards.  Off unless the link defines
__sanitizer_silent_reports; an ordinary build is unchanged.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_printf.cpp | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_printf.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_printf.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_common.h"
 #include "sanitizer_flags.h"
 #include "sanitizer_libc.h"
+#include "sanitizer_silent.h"
 
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdarg.h>
@@ -294,7 +295,15 @@
   // Remove color sequences from the message.
   RemoveANSIEscapeSequencesFromString(buffer);
   CallPrintfAndReportCallback(buffer);
-  LogMessageOnPrintf(buffer);
+  // Silent reports close the runtime-owned report sinks.  The callback above
+  // is the program's own CallPrintfAndReportCallback and is left alone -- it
+  // is program output, not a runtime write.  LogMessageOnPrintf is the runtime
+  // itself writing the report to syslog when log_to_syslog is set, a
+  // runtime-owned sink ReportFile::Write never sees; close it under the same
+  // test, or a silent (or sealed) binary still emits the full report to
+  // /dev/log.  See sanitizer_silent.h.
+  if (!SilentReports())
+    LogMessageOnPrintf(buffer);
 
   va_end(args2);
 }
